    {
      "id": 60,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Read this first",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 11,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 60
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### Read this before trusting these numbers\n\n**Directional, not billing truth.** Both providers are flat-rate subscriptions\n($200/mo-class) -- there is no per-token bill to reconcile against. This row\nanswers \"how many retail-priced dollars of work does each subscription\nwindow deliver\" as a **relative efficiency signal**, not a real cost.\n\n- **Numerator** (retail-$): `claude_code_cost_usage_USD_total` is Claude\n  Code's own list-price approximation -- verified 2026-08-24 by cross-checking\n  a real session's token counts against Anthropic's published API pricing\n  (computed $216.12 vs Claude Code's reported $229.56, within ~6%, consistent\n  with the SAME pricing basis -- see recording-rules.yml for the worked\n  example). Codex's numerator is the Task A ESTIMATED-cost series (same\n  list-price logic, Loki-only -- see the two panels below).\n- **Denominator** (limit consumed): neither provider exports this as OTLP.\n  `ai_subscription_limit_used_percent` is a NEW gauge fed by\n  `scripts/ai-subscription-limit-scraper.sh` (proven live end-to-end\n  2026-08-24) -- not yet reflected in Prometheus until the collector config\n  is deployed (restart required).\n- **Limit-charging weights are opaque.** A model that looks \"cheap per\n  limit-%\" here might be internally weighted differently against the actual\n  subscription quota than its published token price implies -- Anthropic/\n  OpenAI do not publish that mapping.\n- **Reset-boundary handling**: the ratio uses a rolling `[5h]`/`[7d]` lookback,\n  not a window strictly aligned to `resets_at`. Right after a reset,\n  `used_percent` drops to ~0 while the rolling $ window still includes\n  pre-reset spend -- the ratio will read artificially high in that gap.\n  Cross-check `resets_at` (carried on every subscription-limit sample) before\n  trusting a ratio near a reset boundary.\n"
      }
    },
